course: dictionary
lesson: 4639
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word hip can name a part of your body, a kind of joint, a red berry, or even be an informal word for cool. «Hands on hips» is about your body, but «Ben's jacket is so hip» is about fashion.
explain: Read the words around the word first. «Each hip bone looks like one bone but is really three fused together» is about the bones of the body, so hip means the part of your body where your leg joins your waist. Sentences about seat belts and dancing point to the body too.
explain: Some words are only used in set phrases. «Hip, hip, hooray» is a cheer, and people shout it to celebrate someone. «Hip-hop» is a style of music and dance, not a part of your body. When a phrase is famous, learn it as a whole.
fact: Hip is a noun when it means the part of the body where the leg joins the waist.
fact: The hip is a ball-and-socket joint, so the leg can move in many directions.
fact: In everyday talk, hip can also be an informal adjective that means fashionable and cool.
fact: A rose hip is a red berry that grows on a wild rose.
fact: «Hip, hip, hooray» is a cheer that people shout to celebrate someone.
exercise: «Each hip bone looks like one bone but is really three fused together.» How many bones is each hip bone really? | Five | *Three | One | Two
exercise: Ben says, «That jacket is so hip!» What does hip mean here? | A cheer for someone | *Fashionable and cool | A red berry | A part of the body
exercise: Which kind of joint is the hip? | Pivot joint | Hinge joint | *Ball-and-socket joint | Gliding joint
test: «A seat belt's lap part goes low across the hips.» Where does the lap part of a seat belt go? | Around the wrist | Above the eyebrows | Across the neck | *Low across the hips
test: What are people doing when they shout «hip, hip, hooray»? | Asking for help | Running a race | *Cheering someone | Whispering a secret
exam: Which word completes «A rose ___ is a small red berry»? | *hip | hit | hop | hope
